  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Solve $2-3\\sqrt{x}=-x.$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve the equation $2-3\\sqrt{x}=-x,$ we raise both sides to the power of $2.$  This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3\\sqrt{x} & =-x \\\\ -3\\sqrt{x} & =-x-2 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{x} & =x+2 \\\\ {(3\\sqrt{x})}^{2} & =(x+2)^{2} \\\\ 9x & ={x}^{2}+4x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x+4 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-x)+(-4x+4) & =0 \\\\ x(x-1)-4(x-1) & =0 \\\\ (x-4)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\n- First, we check whether the obtained value $x=1$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3\\sqrt{1} & \\overset{?}{=}-1 \\\\ 2-3 & \\overset{?}{=}-1 \\\\ -1 & =-1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the value $x=1$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\n- Second, we check whether the obtained value $x=4$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3\\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}-4 \\\\ 2-6 & \\overset{?}{=}-4 \\\\ -4 & =-4\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the value $x=4$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\nTherefore, the only solution of $2-3\\sqrt{x}=-x$  are $x=1,\\,4.$",
